IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- THE KEEL'S OWN DEFINITIONS: the interpretation provisions that fall in the keel's line
-- ranges (Part XIII: ss 172, 173, 174, and the rule of reading in s 177(2)).
-- Source: a plain-text rendering by a converter script, not the official text.
--
-- Every top-level name below begins with the section it implements, as the part
-- agents' names will, so that nothing here can collide with a part at the merge.
-- A "no" carries a FALSE-kind comment and a "not covered" carries a NOTHING-kind comment
-- (BRIEF, "What FALSE and NOTHING mean"); NOTES.md lists the marginal readings.
--
-- The Act's OTHER definitions sit in the parts' ranges and are theirs: PLAN.md lists them.

§ `Part XIII -- General: interpretation (ss 172, 173, 174, 177(2))`

§§ `s 172 -- Application of Act in relation to territorial waters`

-- src:4334 | In this Act—
-- src:4335 | (a) any reference to Great Britain includes a reference to the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Great Britain;
-- src:4336 | (b) any reference to the United Kingdom includes a reference to the territorial waters of the United Kingdom.
@ref s 172 (src:4334-4336)
DECLARE `s 172 Place` IS ONE OF
    `s 172 the land of Great Britain`
    `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Great Britain`
    `s 172 the land of Northern Ireland`
    `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Northern Ireland`
    `s 172 somewhere else`

-- "includes": the list the Act adds is not closed, so a place it does not name is NOT covered,
-- never "no". The land of Great Britain is Great Britain by the ordinary meaning of a term
-- the Act does not define; s 172 only ADDS the adjacent waters.
@ref s 172(a) (src:4335)
GIVEN `the place` IS A `s 172 Place`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `s 172 the land of Great Britain` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Great Britain` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 the land of Northern Ireland` THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (includes)
    WHEN `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Northern Ireland` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes)
    WHEN `s 172 somewhere else` THEN NOTHING                      -- NOTHING-kind (includes)

@ref s 172(b) (src:4336)
GIVEN `the place` IS A `s 172 Place`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 172(b) a reference to the United Kingdom includes the place` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `s 172 the land of Great Britain` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Great Britain` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 the land of Northern Ireland` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 the territorial waters of the United Kingdom adjacent to Northern Ireland` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 172 somewhere else` THEN NOTHING                      -- NOTHING-kind (includes)

§§ `s 173 -- Age`

-- A person attains a particular age, "expressed in years", at the commencement of the relevant
-- anniversary of the date of birth; the rule is the same in Scotland as in England and Wales,
-- so no nation is asked. The day in question is a date, and the commencement of an anniversary
-- is the first moment of that date.
-- src:4338 | For the purposes of this Act a person—
-- src:4339 | (a) is over or under a particular age if he has or, as the case may be, has not attained that age; and
-- src:4340 | (b) is between two particular ages if he has attained the first but not the second;
-- src:4341 | and in Scotland (as in England and Wales) the time at which a person attains a particular age expressed in years is the commencement of the relevant anniversary of the date of his birth.
@ref s 173 (src:4338-4341)
GIVEN `the age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 173 the age is expressed in years` MEANS
    FLOOR `the age` EQUALS `the age` AND `the age` AT LEAST 0

GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 173 the year has a 29 February` MEANS
        year MODULO 400 EQUALS 0
    OR  (year MODULO 4 EQUALS 0 AND (NOT (year MODULO 100 EQUALS 0)))

-- The anniversary of 29 February in a year with no 29 February is a day the Act does not name
-- (28 February or 1 March): a fork, recorded in NOTES.md, and the day in question decides
-- whether the two readings differ.
@ref s 173 (src:4341)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the age in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 173 the anniversary has no day to fall on` MEANS
        DATE_DAY `the date of birth` EQUALS 29
    AND DATE_MONTH `the date of birth` EQUALS 2
    AND (NOT (`s 173 the year has a 29 February` (DATE_YEAR `the date of birth` PLUS `the age in years`)))

@ref s 173 (src:4341)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the age in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 173 the day the person attains the age` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 173 the age is expressed in years` `the age in years`)
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a particular age expressed in years"
        IF `s 173 the anniversary has no day to fall on` `the date of birth` `the age in years`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): which day is the anniversary of 29 February in a year with none
        OTHERWISE
            JUST (Date (DATE_DAY `the date of birth`) (DATE_MONTH `the date of birth`) (DATE_YEAR `the date of birth` PLUS `the age in years`))

@ref s 173 (src:4341)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the age in years` IS A NUMBER
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 173 the person has attained the age` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 173 the age is expressed in years` `the age in years`)
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a particular age expressed in years"
        IF `s 173 the anniversary has no day to fall on` `the date of birth` `the age in years`
            THEN
                -- a birthday on 29 February: before 28 February of that year both readings say not yet,
                -- from 1 March both say yes, and on 28 February they differ
                BRANCH
                    IF `the day in question` LESS THAN (Date 28 2 `the anniversary year`)
                        THEN JUST FALSE     -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the time at which a person attains a particular age expressed in years is the commencement of the relevant anniversary of the date of his birth"
                    IF `the day in question` AT LEAST (Date 1 3 `the anniversary year`)
                        THEN JUST TRUE
                    OTHERWISE
                        NOTHING         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): 28 February is the anniversary on one reading and not on the other
        OTHERWISE
            IF `the day in question` AT LEAST (Date (DATE_DAY `the date of birth`) (DATE_MONTH `the date of birth`) `the anniversary year`)
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the time at which a person attains a particular age expressed in years is the commencement of the relevant anniversary of the date of his birth"
    WHERE
        `the anniversary year` MEANS DATE_YEAR `the date of birth` PLUS `the age in years`

-- (a): "over" is "has attained", "under" is "has not attained". Each is an "if" that names its
-- own condition, and a failed "if" is counted as the text's no (marginal reading (a), NOTES.md).
@ref s 173(a) (src:4339)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the age in years` IS A NUMBER
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 173(a) the person is over the age` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 173 the person has attained the age` `the date of birth` `the age in years` `the day in question`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING       -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see s 173 the person has attained the age
    WHEN JUST `attained` THEN
        IF `attained`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is over or under a particular age if he has or, as the case may be, has not attained that age"

@ref s 173(a) (src:4339)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the age in years` IS A NUMBER
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 173(a) the person is under the age` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 173 the person has attained the age` `the date of birth` `the age in years` `the day in question`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING       -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see s 173 the person has attained the age
    WHEN JUST `attained` THEN
        IF `attained`
        THEN JUST FALSE             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is over or under a particular age if he has or, as the case may be, has not attained that age"
        ELSE JUST TRUE

@ref s 173(b) (src:4340)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
      `the first age in years` IS A NUMBER
      `the second age in years` IS A NUMBER
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 173(b) the person is between the two ages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 173 the person has attained the age` `the date of birth` `the first age in years` `the day in question`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING       -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see s 173 the person has attained the age
    WHEN JUST `attained the first` THEN
        CONSIDER `s 173 the person has attained the age` `the date of birth` `the second age in years` `the day in question`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): see s 173 the person has attained the age
        WHEN JUST `attained the second` THEN
            IF `attained the first` AND (NOT `attained the second`)
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is between two particular ages if he has attained the first but not the second"

§§ `s 174 -- References to Acts`

-- src:4343 | In this Act—
-- src:4344 | “the 1975 Act” means the Social Security Act 1975;
-- src:4345 | “the 1986 Act” means the Social Security Act 1986;
-- src:4346 | “the Administration Act” means the Social Security Administration Act 1992;
-- src:4347 | “the Consequential Provisions Act” means the Social Security (Consequential Provisions) Act 1992;
-- src:4348 | “the Northern Ireland Contributions and Benefits Act” means the Social Security Contributions and Benefits (Northern Ireland) Act 1992;
-- src:4349 | “the Old Cases Act” means the Industrial Injuries and Diseases (Old Cases) Act 1975; and
-- src:4350 | “the Pensions Act” means the Pension Schemes Act 1993.
@ref s 174 (src:4343-4350)
DECLARE `s 174 Short name` IS ONE OF
    `s 174 the 1975 Act`
    `s 174 the 1986 Act`
    `s 174 the Administration Act`
    `s 174 the Consequential Provisions Act`
    `s 174 the Northern Ireland Contributions and Benefits Act`
    `s 174 the Old Cases Act`
    `s 174 the Pensions Act`

-- "means": each entry is the meaning, exactly. The title is the Act's own words.
@ref s 174 (src:4344-4350)
GIVEN `the short name` IS A `s 174 Short name`
GIVETH A STRING
`s 174 the title of the Act meant by` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the short name`
    WHEN `s 174 the 1975 Act` THEN "Social Security Act 1975"
    WHEN `s 174 the 1986 Act` THEN "Social Security Act 1986"
    WHEN `s 174 the Administration Act` THEN "Social Security Administration Act 1992"
    WHEN `s 174 the Consequential Provisions Act` THEN "Social Security (Consequential Provisions) Act 1992"
    WHEN `s 174 the Northern Ireland Contributions and Benefits Act` THEN "Social Security Contributions and Benefits (Northern Ireland) Act 1992"
    WHEN `s 174 the Old Cases Act` THEN "Industrial Injuries and Diseases (Old Cases) Act 1975"
    WHEN `s 174 the Pensions Act` THEN "Pension Schemes Act 1993"

-- The other way round: the section lists seven short names and does not say it lists all
-- the Acts there are, so a title it does not give a short name to is NOT covered.
@ref s 174 (src:4344-4350)
GIVEN `the title` IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 174 Short name`
`s 174 the short name given to the Act titled` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Social Security Act 1975" THEN JUST `s 174 the 1975 Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Social Security Act 1986" THEN JUST `s 174 the 1986 Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Social Security Administration Act 1992" THEN JUST `s 174 the Administration Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Social Security (Consequential Provisions) Act 1992" THEN JUST `s 174 the Consequential Provisions Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Social Security Contributions and Benefits (Northern Ireland) Act 1992" THEN JUST `s 174 the Northern Ireland Contributions and Benefits Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Industrial Injuries and Diseases (Old Cases) Act 1975" THEN JUST `s 174 the Old Cases Act`
        IF `the title` EQUALS "Pension Schemes Act 1993" THEN JUST `s 174 the Pensions Act`
        OTHERWISE
            NOTHING         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the section gives short names to seven Acts and says nothing of any other

§§ `s 177(2) -- the Act is to be read with two other Acts`

-- Only the rule of reading in s 177(2) is encoded here. The rest of s 177 is machinery (NOTES.md).
-- "where appropriate" hands the judgment to the reader: it is a plain input, and when it is
-- not met the section's grant is limited by its own wording, so the answer is NOT covered.
-- src:4446 | (2) This Act is to be read, where appropriate, with the Administration Act and the Consequential Provisions Act.
@ref s 177(2) (src:4446)
GIVEN `the other Act` IS A `s 174 Short name`
      `the reading is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 177(2) this Act is to be read with the other Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the other Act`
    WHEN `s 174 the Administration Act` THEN
        IF `the reading is appropriate`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "where appropriate"
    WHEN `s 174 the Consequential Provisions Act` THEN
        IF `the reading is appropriate`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "where appropriate"
    WHEN `s 174 the 1975 Act` THEN NOTHING     -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN `s 174 the 1986 Act` THEN NOTHING     -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN `s 174 the Northern Ireland Contributions and Benefits Act` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN `s 174 the Old Cases Act` THEN NOTHING     -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN `s 174 the Pensions Act` THEN NOTHING      -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
